  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies entirely on a small sample of self-reported walking behavior rather than objective physical activity tracking.",
      "what": "A prospective correlational study evaluated the Theory of Planned Behaviour's ability to forecast parent-child joint walking frequency across school, non-school, and leisure trips using online surveys administered one month apart to 128 parents in Scotland. The framework explained 33% to 76% of the variance in actual walking frequency and 36% to 69% of the variance in walking intentions. Across all three trip types, behavioral intentions and perceived behavioral control directly predicted walking occurrences, while attitudes and control perceptions—but not social norms—consistently predicted intentions.",
      "why": "The findings establish that interventions aiming to boost family walking should target parental attitudes and confidence rather than social pressure, providing a framework for active transport campaigns."
    }
  },
